+# Upkeep traps: why Rome had no Writing at 410 BC
+
+Follow-up to `MASINISSA_AUTORUN_FIXES.md`, from the same Marathon autorun save
+(`Masinissa BC-0410`, game turn 269), replayed headlessly with the real DLL in
+the GertaBool/RoM `toolchain/harness`. The full investigation, with logs, is in
+that repository under `research/rome-and-settlers`.
+
+## What happened to Rome
+
+The save keeps per-turn history. Rome's total commerce stayed between 16 and 37
+from turn 0 to turn 260 while other civilizations grew four- to tenfold. It
+founded its second and third city on turns 106 and 124 and its fourth on turn
+260. On turn 144 it adopted Mercenaries and kept it. At 410 BC it had 4 cities
+and 45 units (14 of them scouts); unit charges took 45 of its 96 commerce, the
+AI was in financial trouble, the slider stood at 95 percent gold, research made
+3 beakers a turn, and the capital logged an open settlement site (value 3777)
+with a settler allowance of 0 because of the financial trouble. Rome had
+Priesthood and Trade, so Writing was available; it was never affordable.
+
+The same pattern (Mercenaries, the highest unit charges, financial trouble)
+holds for every civilization still on Mercenaries at 410 BC.
+
+## Causes in the current source
+
+1. `AI_civicValue`, free experience: `experience * population * 30%` (12% in
+   peace) times the production multiplier. For Rome the five points were worth
+   45 a turn, half its commerce, and more than the 31 gold a turn the civic's
+   unit charges cost. Now: the promotions the experience buys (BTS thresholds:
+   five points are two promotions), at 15 percent of a unit each, on the hammers
+   that go into military units (two fifths of production with a war plan, one
+   fifth otherwise).
+2. `AI_civicValue`, unit charges: the projected cost was the raw per-unit
+   charge. `calculateUnitCost` multiplies it by the handicap's unit cost percent
+   and, for AIs, by the AI unit cost and per-era percents, and inflation applies
+   to all costs. Now the projection is in that gold.
+3. `AI_civicValue`, happiness per military unit: three units were assumed in
+   every city. Now the cities' actual average is used.
+4. `AI_disbandUnit(1, ...)`: only units with at most one experience point could
+   be disbanded. Units trained under Mercenaries have five at birth, so an AI in
+   financial trouble had nothing it could disband. Now the threshold counts from
+   the experience a unit of that type would be trained with today.
+5. `AI_exploreMove`: a surplus explorer (more in the area than
+   `AI_neededExplorers`) was scrapped only after the search for something to
+   explore failed, which on a large map never happens. Rome kept 14 scouts. The
+   check now comes first.
+
+Not changed: `AI_doCivics` weighs a switch over a horizon of twice
+`AI_CIVIC_CHANGE_DELAY` turns at every speed while anarchy lasts twice as long
+on Marathon. Scaling the horizon with the anarchy percent was tried; over 40
+turns from the save (six seeds) it raised anarchy from 34 to 46 player-turns
+without a measurable gain, so it needs a longer study first.
+
+## Settlers
+
+Over the whole game 141 settlers were created (18 at the start) and 122 cities
+founded; 18 settlers were alive at 410 BC. Settlers were not built faster than
+they were used; about one in eight was standing idle, for the reasons patch
+`MASINISSA_AUTORUN_FIXES.md` addresses (sites behind foreign borders counted for
+production, no retirement, no escort, a transport waiting at sea). Over 30 turns
+from the save, the code before that patch trained 27 settlers for 20 new cities
+and ended with 21 waiting; the current code trains 32 for 26 and ends with 17,
+mostly younger than 17 turns. Willem's two settlers in a border city under
+constant threat still wait.
+
+## Tests
+
+`Tools/test_upkeep_traps.py`: the civic terms with Rome's numbers,
+`AI_disbandUnit` compiled with stand-ins (a unit with its birth experience is
+disbanded, a veteran is not), and the order of the explorer check.
